WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Do you remember hurricanes or floods when you were growing up in the Ninth Ward?

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:04.560
Yeah.

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.920
It didn't seem like there was as many hurricanes as now.

4
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:15.920
But there were multiple ones in the 1960s I remember.

5
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.860
Beulah [1967] and Hilda happened pre-1965.

6
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.120
Hilda blew down a pecan tree in the yard, so I remember it.

7
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:34.420
And then Betsy was September 9, 1965, and it flooded the Lower Ninth Ward.

8
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:40.340
Flooded my yard until about three feet deep and maybe a little less than a foot in the house.

9
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:52.400
It drowned some of my chickens, other ones flew up and got away from it.

10
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.720
Grandpa stayed with his dog in the car in a part that didn't flood.

11
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.860
We went to a shelter in the Upper Ninth Ward in a high school.

12
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.980
Could you explain why he stayed with his dog?

13
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.640
Well, they wouldn't let his dog in the shelter, so he just sat out in the car.

14
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:16.280
The winds were supposedly 130 miles an hour.

15
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.920
I know they were strong.

16
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.220
I saw lots of windows and doors blow out in the school.

17
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.000
When you're in the eye wall, like with Betsy, it's just constant wind.

18
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.960
There's just some variation on how strong it is.

19
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:41.680
Houses rock, there's always a roar from all the wind encountering and moving things.

20
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.800
It just turns into a low frequency roar.
